**How to approach any 'predict the output' question:** identify the static type of the pointer/reference, the dynamic type of the object, whether the function is `virtual`, and whether the call goes through a pointer/reference or a copy.

```cpp
struct Base {
  virtual void f() { cout << "Base::f\n"; }
  void g()         { cout << "Base::g\n"; }
  virtual ~Base() {}
};
struct Derived : Base {
  void f() override { cout << "Derived::f\n"; }
  void g()          { cout << "Derived::g\n"; }
};
Base* p = new Derived;
p->f();   // Derived::f  (virtual: resolved at run time via the vtable)
p->g();   // Base::g     (non-virtual: resolved at compile time from the static type)
Base b = Derived();
b.f();    // Base::f     (object slicing: b is a Base)
```

**Why.** A virtual call uses the object's vptr to look up the function in its class's vtable, so the dynamic type decides. A non-virtual call is bound by the static type, so `Derived::g` merely *hides* `Base::g`. Copying a Derived into a Base value slices off the derived part.

**Common traps to check in the code:**
- A signature that differs slightly (const, parameter type) means it *hides* rather than overrides. `override` makes the compiler catch this.
- **Default arguments** are bound statically: a virtual call can run `Derived::f` with `Base`'s default value.
- Virtual calls inside a constructor or destructor dispatch to the class currently being built, not the most-derived one.
- A missing virtual destructor makes `delete p` on a base pointer undefined behaviour.
- Private overrides are still called through a base pointer, because access is checked statically.

